#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138069 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138510 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54498b47-9a43-5b4d-8bbf-806af5968829" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Fractions with Like Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "e85da1d1-1cf3-547c-86ce-42329475fbdd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12835" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}k +1 + \\dfrac{4}{3}k - 3$." :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}k + 1 + \\frac{4}{3}k - 3 &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{3}k + \\frac{4}{3}k) + (1 - 3) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1 + 4}{3})k + (-2) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138070 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dec4e22a-5882-56d9-8e13-b18b4d22efdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Distributive Law With Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "30cf0891-33d3-566c-8286-4c161f84c1dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12836" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{2}(p-4q)$." :question/worked-solution "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}(p - 4q) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot p - (\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot 4q &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}p - \\frac{4q}{2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}p - 2q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138071 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75d096f5-9834-5e52-a7eb-9aae72398d19" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Fractions with Unlike Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "53c1f7ca-bcaa-59e4-9929-0695b67621cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12837" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $-\\dfrac{1}{2}u + \\dfrac{7}{8}u$." :question/worked-solution "We combine the like terms by adding the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{1}{2}u + \\frac{7}{8}u &=  \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{7}{8})u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the coefficients, we have to convert them to have the same denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{7}{8})u &=  \\\\\n(-\\frac{4}{8} + \\frac{7}{8})u &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{-4 + 7}{8})u &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}u\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138072 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138513 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3e39a9d-1f97-54f2-a388-90c71c83b93e" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Distributive Law"}] :question/id #uuid "957b3102-6eb7-52d3-9299-54ed9bd4718e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12892" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}(3a+6b)+2a$." :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the distributive law.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}(3a + 6b) + 2a &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot 3a + (\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot 6b + 2a &=  \\\\\n\\frac{3a}{3} + \\frac{6b}{3} + 2a &=  \\\\\n&= a + 2b + 2a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2b + 2a &=  \\\\\na + 2a + 2b &=  \\\\\n&= 3a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dec4e22a-5882-56d9-8e13-b18b4d22efdc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Distributive Law With Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba5240af-888b-5cee-85bd-66dee3fad1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 3y + 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135531} {:answer/id #uuid "bedec537-d439-582d-9134-0766d2835c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + y - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135532} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5133ba-43a6-5eee-bee4-0003545c04ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/m